[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ng4 {Black goes for 7...Ng4 and attacks the e5-pawn twice. We'll defend it with a developing move, Re1.} 8.Re1 8...b5 {Black attacks our bishop in return. We retreat to b3.} 9.Bb3 9...d6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and challenges our e5-pawn. In return, they've weakened their light-squares on he queenside. We'll exploit the weakened light-squares with Bd5, pinning the knight to the rook.} 10.Bd5 10...Qd7 {Black defends on c6 with the queen. But falls for a tactic! The f7-pawn proves to be a weakness here.} 11.Bxf7+ 11...Kxf7 {Black grabs the bishop.} 12.e6+ {And now we win the queen!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ne4 {Black plays actively and jumps to the e4-square. This is the best move and also the most popular. It's time to grab the d4-pawn, as failing to do so could hinder our development. We can do this because 8...Nxd4 9.Qxd4 comes with a tempo on the knight on e4.} 8.Nxd4 8...Nc5 {Black retreats their knight to c5 and attacks our bishop on a4. We don't have to worry about our bishop! Nf5 is a potent move, which creates a threat of a much higher level.} 9.Nf5 9...O-O {Black sees through our threat and castles. Now we'll play Qg4 and threaten a checkmate on g7.} 10.Qg4 10...g6 {Black defends with ...g6. We'll lose the bishop anyway, so let's trade it off on c6 and double up Black's c-pawns.} 11.Bxc6 11...dxc6 {Black recaptures. To weaken the dark-squares, we'll trade off Black's bishop on e7.} 12.Nxe7+ {To weaken the dark-squares, we'll trade off Black's bishop on e7.} 12...Qxe7 {Black recaptures.} 13.Qg3 {The dark squares around Black's king are seriously weak. Re1 and Nc3 are good plans that you can never go wrong with, and in some positions b3 and Ba3 is a good idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ne4 {Black plays actively and jumps to the e4-square. This is the best move and also the most popular. It's time to grab the d4-pawn, as failing to do so could hinder our development. We can do this because 8...Nxd4 9.Qxd4 comes with a tempo on the knight on e4.} 8.Nxd4 8...Nc5 {Black retreats their knight to c5 and attacks our bishop on a4. We don't have to worry about our bishop! Nf5 is a potent move, which creates a threat of a much higher level.} 9.Nf5 9...Nxa4 {What happens if Black goes for the material? We'll capture on g7 with check!} 10.Nxg7+ 10...Kf8 {The king has to move to f8. Now we'll bring our bishop out and set up a discovered check.} 11.Bh6 11...Kg8 {Black moves away from the bishop's eye.} 12.f4 {f4 is just crushing here. It allows our rook to join the attack while Black's queenside is sleeping.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ne4 {Black plays actively and jumps to the e4-square. This is the best move and also the most popular. It's time to grab the d4-pawn, as failing to do so could hinder our development. We can do this because 8...Nxd4 9.Qxd4 comes with a tempo on the knight on e4.} 8.Nxd4 8...O-O {Black gets their king into safety. Our rook will develop to the useful e1-square and attack Black's knight.} 9.Re1 9...Nc5 {Black retreats to c5, essentially getting rid of our light-squared bishop. We'll trade it off for the c6-knight, which was pressuring our e5-pawn.} 10.Bxc6 10...dxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll get the knight developed to c3. Our pieces have a lot of freedom — open lines that will allow for a future attack.} 11.Nc3 11...Ne6 {Black offers to trade off our strong centralized knight on d4. We'll decline of course, and bring our knight to a strong square, closer to Black's king.} 12.Nf5 12...Bc5 {Black saves the bishop (12...Qxd1 13.Nxe7+ wins a piece) but 12...Bc5 is a blunder, as normal as it looks. 12...Bg5 or 12...Nc5 are better, computer choices. Your opponent will have a hard time finding these plans! The queen is a menacing piece on g4. It supports the attack with Ne4 and Bh6.} 13.Qg4 13...g6 {Black tries to place the g7-pawn where it will be less of a target.} 14.Ne4 {After Ne4, Black's position is difficult to defend. Ideas of Nf6+ are in the air, with Rd1 controlling the d-file and the dark-squared bishop controlling the weak dark squares. The attack cannot be stopped. This is +3 for White. This shows that, after very natural moves, if Black doesn't know what they're doing they could all of a sudden be lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ne4 {Black plays actively and jumps to the e4-square. This is the best move and also the most popular. It's time to grab the d4-pawn, as failing to do so could hinder our development. We can do this because 8...Nxd4 9.Qxd4 comes with a tempo on the knight on e4.} 8.Nxd4 8...O-O {Black gets their king into safety. Our rook will develop to the useful e1-square and attack Black's knight.} 9.Re1 9...Nxd4 {Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 10.Qxd4 10...Nc5 {The knight saves itself by jumping to c5. We'll develop the knight to a natural square.} 11.Nc3 11...d6 {Black challenges our e-pawn.} 12.Bb3 {The bishop avoids being traded on a4. Black doesn't face any immediate threats at the moment, but White has better control over the center and more space to work with. We'll likely get an open a-file after a trade on b3, and our c3-knight can go to e4 or d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black chooses to jump to d5 instead, the second most popular move with the knight. We'll capture the d-pawn now that it is safe.} 8.Nxd4 8...Nxd4 {This trade is popular and commonly seen, but it is also bad for Black. Better was 8...0-0. We'll recapture and get the queen centralized.} 9.Qxd4 9...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight and attacks our bishop. But their knight will not be an active piece on b6. The bishop retreats to b3 and eyes the weak f7-pawn.} 10.Bb3 10...O-O {Black castles.} 11.Nc3 {After developing with Nc3 and bringing out the bishop to f4, we'll be ready to control the central files with Rd1 and Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 1: Black plays 5...exd4, 6...Be7 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1877e744-4484-4651-9a11-9abbca8c5b1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ng4 {Black goes for 7...Ng4 and attacks the e5-pawn twice. We'll defend it with a developing move, Re1.} 8.Re1 8...b5 {Black attacks our bishop in return. We retreat to b3.} 9.Bb3 9...O-O {Black might get castled instead. Let's just kick away the knight with h3.} 10.h3 10...Nh6 {This is the only available square for the knight. Which means that we can capture it and damage Black's pawn structure in front of their king!} 11.Bxh6 11...gxh6 {Black recaptures. With c3, we either grab the pawn on d4 next with cxd4 and get a strong center or Black will trade on c3 and help us develop.} 12.c3 12...dxc3 {Black trades.} 13.Nxc3 {All of our pieces have nice squares while Black's king feels very unsafe. We will play Qd2 and we have constant pressure on f7. We have an almost +2 position. 13...Bb7 14.Qd2 Bg5 15.Nxg5 Qxg5 16.f4 is not good for Black.} *

