1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its most natural square. This move develops a piece and attacks the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing and protecting the e5-pawn, which is the most popular move here, with 63% occurrence. We'll develop another piece with 3.Bb5, which signifies the Spanish/Ruy Lopez. The main idea is to attack the defender of e5.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our b5-bishop a question: do you retreat or trade for the knight? This is the Morphy Defense, the most popular at both the amateur and master level. 25% of games under 2000 go like this, and more than 70% at master level. We'll retreat to a4, maintaining the pressure on the knight.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight and puts some pressure on the e4-pawn, which is played nearly 50% of the time. We'll play d4. This is the signature move of the Aggressive Ruy Lopez! Instead of slow maneuvering, White immediately strikes in the center and poses some questions to Black. If Black allows it, our idea will be to push e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black chooses to trade off the central pawns in nearly half of the games. Let's get the king into safety before taking more action in the center. We should also be careful about capturing on d4 right away since Black has ideas of pushing ...c5, ..b5 and ...c4 to trap our bishop.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside. Now we'll push forward with e5, gaining some central space and attacking the knight.} 7.e5 7...Ng4 {Black goes for 7...Ng4 and attacks the e5-pawn twice. We'll defend it with a developing move, Re1.} 8.Re1 8...b5 {Black attacks our bishop in return. We retreat to b3.} 9.Bb3 9...d6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and challenges our e5-pawn. In return, they've weakened their light-squares on he queenside. We'll exploit the weakened light-squares with Bd5, pinning the knight to the rook.} 10.Bd5 10...Bb7 {Black supports the knight.} 11.Nxd4 {But after Nxd4 White has a winning position. Both of Black's knights are under a lot of pressure!} *

