1.e4 1...e5 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 3...a6 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 5.d4 5...exd4 6.O-O 6...Nxe4 7.Re1 7...d5 8.Nxd4 8...Bd7 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 10.f3 *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxd4 {In a mere 4% of the games, Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll trade so that e5 is possible.} 6.Nxd4 6...exd4 {Black recaptures the material. Now we'll get in the thematic e5 push, which gains space in the center and kicks the knight away from f6.} 7.e5 7...Qe7 {Black defends their knight by pinning our e-pawn.} 8.O-O {So we just unpin by castling! Black has got problems along the e-file. Remember the ideas we've seen: c3, Nxc3 and Qf3. If the knight goes to the center and lets you attack it with a tempo, Qxd4 is playable. Otherwise, be careful with Qxd4 and getting your bishop trapped.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxd4 {In a mere 4% of the games, Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll trade so that e5 is possible.} 6.Nxd4 6...exd4 {Black recaptures the material. Now we'll get in the thematic e5 push, which gains space in the center and kicks the knight away from f6.} 7.e5 7...Ne4 {The knight jumps to the e4-square. We'll recapture on d4, which also comes with an attack on the knight.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nc5 {So Black jumps their knight back to c5. We'll simply continue developing our pieces.} 9.Nc3 9...Nxa4 {Black gets rid of our bishop pair. Losing the bishop pair isn't a problem here. Our edge relies on having a spatial advantage.} 10.Qxa4 10...Bc5 {Black develops the bishop.} 11.Qg4 {And now that Black's dark-squared bishop has left the kingside, we'll shift our queen there and prepare to launch an attack!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxe4 {This move has been played in 10% of the games. Black immediately grabs our e-pawn. The queen moves to e2 and eyes the uncastled king. The most popular move 6...d5 is a mistake. The correct move is 6...b5.} 6.Qe2 6...d5 {Black defends the knight with ...d5. But they forget about the knight on c6! Now we regain the pawn and gang up on the c6-knight by playing Nxe5. What else does this move secretly do? It clears a file...} 7.Nxe5 7...Bd7 {Black defends the knight. Now we have a similar tactic to the previous study. First we trade off the knights.} 8.Nxc6 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures.} 9.f3 {And now we win the e4-knight due to the pin.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxe4 {This move has been played in 10% of the games. Black immediately grabs our e-pawn. The queen moves to e2 and eyes the uncastled king. The most popular move 6...d5 is a mistake. The correct move is 6...b5.} 6.Qe2 6...b5 {Black opts for 6...b5 to attack our bishop on a4. This is the right move because it breaks the pin. We'll grab the knight. The queen is powerful in the center.} 7.Qxe4 7...bxa4 {Black regains the piece. We'll gain space with d5 and attack the knight at the same time.} 8.d5 8...Ne7 {The knight retreats to e7.} 9.d6 {And d6 is a double attack on the knight and the rook on a8. White is simply winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxe4 {This move has been played in 10% of the games. Black immediately grabs our e-pawn. The queen moves to e2 and eyes the uncastled king. The most popular move 6...d5 is a mistake. The correct move is 6...b5.} 6.Qe2 6...b5 {Black opts for 6...b5 to attack our bishop on a4. This is the right move because it breaks the pin. We'll grab the knight. The queen is powerful in the center.} 7.Qxe4 7...d5 {Black gains a tempo on our queen. We have to retreat. e3 is a good square since the queen stays on the e-file.} 8.Qe3 8...bxa4 {Black grabs the bishop.} 9.Nxe5 {And after Nxe5 we enter a comfortable position where Black has weird pawn islands on the queenside. It's about equal, but after the knights are traded on e5 (9...Nxe5 10.Qxe5+ Be6), we will have sound development with Bf4 and Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Morphy Defense (3...a6) / Study 3: Black plays 5...Nxe4 or 5...Nxd4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/74b1ca51-605f-4793-9351-3eb2529ec140/studies/1e3efdff-d89a-44f4-a41e-1275cb0a040a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We develop a piece and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. We'll retreat to a4 to keep the bishop actively placed.} 4.Ba4 4...Nf6 {Black keeps on developing. And now it's time for the signature move — d4.} 5.d4 5...Nxd4 {In a mere 4% of the games, Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll trade so that e5 is possible.} 6.Nxd4 6...exd4 {Black recaptures the material. Now we'll get in the thematic e5 push, which gains space in the center and kicks the knight away from f6.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black saves the knight by jumping to d5. Now we recapture the d-pawn and get our queen nicely centralized. We can only take this pawn when we can do it with a tempo.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nb6 {The knight retreats to b6.} 9.Nc3 {The e-pawn provides a nice space advantage. Combined with our lead in the development, White is doing much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 1: Black plays 6...exd4 or 6...Nxe4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/f5892d8e-456a-4587-9e74-acc9d53ef3d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll develop the knight to its usual f3-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. Now b5 is a great square for the bishop, placing some pressure on the knight which fights for the central squares.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll retreat it to a4 to retain its influence.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {In 32% of the games, Black expands with ...b5 to gain even more time on the bishop. We'll just retreat to b3. The bishop is aimed nicely at the weak f7-pawn.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black starts to develop the kingside with ...Nf6. Even here, we don't abandon the d4 idea! This is a surprising move to face as Black, especially when there are many moves to choose from.} 6.d4 6...exd4 {Black opts for ...exd4, which is what we hoped for since it allows e5. It's also the most popular move, occurring in nearly 50% of the games. Let's get the king into safety first before starting any action in the center.} 7.O-O 7...Bc5 {Black supports the d4-pawn. But e5 is especially strong when Black's king is still in the center and ours is safely castled.} 8.e5 8...Ng4 {The knight jumps to g4 and attacks our e5-pawn. Now c3 is a powerful move, setting up a pawn fork on d4 if Black captures our e-pawn.} 9.c3 9...dxc3 {If Black tries to keep the extra pawn by capturing on c3... First a powerful intermezzo! The queen threatens checkmate on f7 as well as the bishop on c5 and occupies a strong central square, where it cannot be kicked out.} 10.Qd5 10...Qe7 {Black defends against checkmate and saves the bishop.} 11.Nxc3 {We are doing great after Nxc3. All of our pieces are active and Black has a hard time finding a good developing plan. We are winning. 11...h6 12.Qe4 puts Black in a difficult spot: if they have to play ...Nxf2 that won't end well for them.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 1: Black plays 6...exd4 or 6...Nxe4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/f5892d8e-456a-4587-9e74-acc9d53ef3d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll develop the knight to its usual f3-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. Now b5 is a great square for the bishop, placing some pressure on the knight which fights for the central squares.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll retreat it to a4 to retain its influence.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {In 32% of the games, Black expands with ...b5 to gain even more time on the bishop. We'll just retreat to b3. The bishop is aimed nicely at the weak f7-pawn.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black starts to develop the kingside with ...Nf6. Even here, we don't abandon the d4 idea! This is a surprising move to face as Black, especially when there are many moves to choose from.} 6.d4 6...Nxe4 {15% of the games follow 6...Nxe4. Black goes after the other "free" central pawn. We'll regain the pawn by capturing on e5 and most importantly opening up the d-file.} 7.dxe5 7...Bc5 {Black puts pressure on f2 instead. This is a losing variation that many people fall for. We'll bring the queen to d5, from where it takes aim at multiple targets.} 8.Qd5 8...Bxf2+ {Black can grab on f2 now. But our king will be safe on e2.} 9.Ke2 9...O-O {Black castles to avoid getting checkmated.. ...but the knight falls!} 10.Qxe4 10...Bb6 {The bishop was attacked as well so Black retreats it.} 11.Nc3 {Continuing to develop with Nc3. We are up a piece and should convert this position easily.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 1: Black plays 6...exd4 or 6...Nxe4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/f5892d8e-456a-4587-9e74-acc9d53ef3d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll develop the knight to its usual f3-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. Now b5 is a great square for the bishop, placing some pressure on the knight which fights for the central squares.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll retreat it to a4 to retain its influence.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {In 32% of the games, Black expands with ...b5 to gain even more time on the bishop. We'll just retreat to b3. The bishop is aimed nicely at the weak f7-pawn.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black starts to develop the kingside with ...Nf6. Even here, we don't abandon the d4 idea! This is a surprising move to face as Black, especially when there are many moves to choose from.} 6.d4 6...exd4 {Black opts for ...exd4, which is what we hoped for since it allows e5. It's also the most popular move, occurring in nearly 50% of the games. Let's get the king into safety first before starting any action in the center.} 7.O-O 7...Nxe4 {Black could also grab the e4-pawn immediately. We'll deploy our rook to the e-file where it targets the uncastled king.} 8.Re1 8...d5 {Black supports the knight. Now we have the beautiful move Nc3, developing the knight right under the pawn's view. The point of this sacrifice is to open up lines for our other pieces which are all beautifully placed.} 9.Nc3 9...dxc3 {Black accepts the sacrifice. The knight on e4 was under too much pressure. 9...Be6 was the only move to keep the game in the balance. Everything else, 9...dxc3 included, is losing for Black. Now we play 10.Bxd5, and both black knights are hanging.} 10.Bxd5 10...Bb7 {Black defends the c6-knight. We'll regain the material.} 11.Bxe4 11...Qxd1 {By trading the queens, Black fights for their king safety. But an intermezzo double-check is our next idea! Black has to move their king now.} 12.Bxc6+ 12...Kd8 {The king steps aside.} 13.Rxd1+ {Now we recapture the queen with yet another check. A simple conversion should follow as we are up a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 1: Black plays 6...exd4 or 6...Nxe4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/f5892d8e-456a-4587-9e74-acc9d53ef3d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll develop the knight to its usual f3-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. Now b5 is a great square for the bishop, placing some pressure on the knight which fights for the central squares.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll retreat it to a4 to retain its influence.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {In 32% of the games, Black expands with ...b5 to gain even more time on the bishop. We'll just retreat to b3. The bishop is aimed nicely at the weak f7-pawn.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black starts to develop the kingside with ...Nf6. Even here, we don't abandon the d4 idea! This is a surprising move to face as Black, especially when there are many moves to choose from.} 6.d4 6...exd4 {Black opts for ...exd4, which is what we hoped for since it allows e5. It's also the most popular move, occurring in nearly 50% of the games. Let's get the king into safety first before starting any action in the center.} 7.O-O 7...d6 {Black tries to play conservatively with ...d6. But we can gain an advantage. We'll play Ng5, when it's difficult to cover the f7-pawn.} 8.Ng5 8...Ne5 {Black defends with the knight. So we'll kick it out with f4!} 9.f4 9...Nc4 {Black blocks the bishop's view of the f7-square.} 10.e5 {And we'll push e5 to open things up in the center and gang up on the f7-pawn in the future. We are borderline winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 1: Black plays 6...exd4 or 6...Nxe4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/f5892d8e-456a-4587-9e74-acc9d53ef3d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll develop the knight to its usual f3-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. Now b5 is a great square for the bishop, placing some pressure on the knight which fights for the central squares.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll retreat it to a4 to retain its influence.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {In 32% of the games, Black expands with ...b5 to gain even more time on the bishop. We'll just retreat to b3. The bishop is aimed nicely at the weak f7-pawn.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black starts to develop the kingside with ...Nf6. Even here, we don't abandon the d4 idea! This is a surprising move to face as Black, especially when there are many moves to choose from.} 6.d4 6...Nxe4 {15% of the games follow 6...Nxe4. Black goes after the other "free" central pawn. We'll regain the pawn by capturing on e5 and most importantly opening up the d-file.} 7.dxe5 7...Nc5 {Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. This is the one move that isn't a mistake, but we can still get a nice position.} 8.Bd5 {Let's not give the bishop up and instead pin the knight on c6 in return. Our idea is to get the king into safety, finish the queenside development, and break the queenside structure with a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...Nxd4 {In 12% of the games, Black captures the d-pawn here. We should trade, so that e5 is next!} 7.Nxd4 7...exd4 {Black recaptures. Funnily, the only move to keep the game in the balance for Black is the Stockfish-approved 7...c5. Not an easy move to find. Now, however, we'll get our e5-move in. Our advantage is substantial.} 8.e5 8...Qe7 {Black saves the knight by pinning the pawn. But this is losing. We often answer a ...Qe7 pin by castling.} 9.O-O 9...Ng8 {This is the only safe square! Now our queen prepares to put pressure on the f7-pawn, and attacks the rook as well.} 10.Qf3 10...Rb8 {Black saves it. But now Bg5 is very disruptive. The black queen cannot leave e7 from where it covers the f7-pawn.} 11.Bg5 11...f6 {Black has to defend with the ugly-looking ...f6. We'll trade to open up the e-file.} 12.exf6 12...gxf6 {Black recaptures, hitting our bishop...} 13.Nd2 {...but we ignore the threat and connect the rooks! Re1 is coming. Game over. Well, you still have to convert.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {In 14% of the games, Black supports the e5-pawn with ...d6. Now 7.c3 is objectively better, but trading on e5 gives us more chances for some quick wins.} 7.dxe5 7...dxe5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Trading the queens is best here, since it disrupts Black's coordination.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Nxd8 {The knight recaptures so they don't lose castling rights. Now the pawn on e5 should be captured to access the f7-pawn later.} 9.Nxe5 9...Nxe4 {Black captures the e4-pawn. We would like to play Bd5 with a fork, but ...Bb7 in reply would save Black. Let's first distract the knight on d8, so that ...Bb7 isn't possible. We'll play Nxf7!} 10.Nxf7 10...Nxf7 {Black recaptures. And now Bd5 is a double attack on the knight and the rook.} 11.Bd5 11...Nxf2 {Black goes for a desperado tactic.} 12.Rf1 {But Rf1 is a strong response, since the knight on f7 is a target as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {In 14% of the games, Black supports the e5-pawn with ...d6. Now 7.c3 is objectively better, but trading on e5 gives us more chances for some quick wins.} 7.dxe5 7...Nxe5 {Black captures on e5 with the knight. We'll trade on e5 and follow up with a tactic.} 8.Nxe5 8...dxe5 {Black recaptures... ...and the bishop shows its power!} 9.Bxf7+ 9...Kxf7 {Black goes for the bishop. The queen isn't completely free...} 10.Qxd8 10...Bb4+ {...Black has this check on b4, opening up an attack on our queen. We'll retreat the queen and block the check.} 11.Qd2 11...Bxd2+ {Black regains the queen.} 12.Nxd2 {And after we recapture the bishop, White is up a pawn going into the endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {In 14% of the games, Black supports the e5-pawn with ...d6. Now 7.c3 is objectively better, but trading on e5 gives us more chances for some quick wins.} 7.dxe5 7...Nxe5 {Black captures on e5 with the knight. We'll trade on e5 and follow up with a tactic.} 8.Nxe5 8...dxe5 {Black recaptures... ...and the bishop shows its power!} 9.Bxf7+ 9...Ke7 {If the king goes to e7... We'll trade to save the bishop.} 10.Qxd8+ 10...Kxd8 {Black regains the queen.} 11.Nc3 {And we simply develop and enjoy a comfortable position with a pawn up.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {In 14% of the games, Black supports the e5-pawn with ...d6. Now 7.c3 is objectively better, but trading on e5 gives us more chances for some quick wins.} 7.dxe5 7...dxe5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Trading the queens is best here, since it disrupts Black's coordination.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Nxd8 {The knight recaptures so they don't lose castling rights. Now the pawn on e5 should be captured to access the f7-pawn later.} 9.Nxe5 9...Bd6 {...Bd6 attacks our knight. We'll retreat to d3, threatening a fork with e5. } 10.Nd3 10...Nxe4 {So Black goes after the e-pawn.} 11.O-O {We'll just get a comfortable position after castling kingside, with Re1 coming and developing the rest of the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Morphy with 4...b5 / Study 2: Black plays 6...d6 or 6...Nxd4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/a94c2f08-2062-4bb8-a479-5c014ff2ac51/studies/85f03953-fc59-4acf-8704-3d6f0514fa8b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us. Now we'll develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends with the knight. We'll develop the bishop to b5, as per usual.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black goes for the Morphy Defense. Our bishop retreats.} 4.Ba4 4...b5 {Black expands even further with ...b5, as they do in 32% of the games. We'll bring our bishop back to b3.} 5.Bb3 5...Nf6 {Black gets the other knight out. And now it's time for the aggressive d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {In 14% of the games, Black supports the e5-pawn with ...d6. Now 7.c3 is objectively better, but trading on e5 gives us more chances for some quick wins.} 7.dxe5 7...dxe5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Trading the queens is best here, since it disrupts Black's coordination.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Kxd8 {Black captures the queen with the king.} 9.Ng5 {But now the f7-pawn is very tender.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...b5 {In nearly 70% of the games, Black pushes ...b5 here and kicks out our bishop from a4. We retreat to b3, from where we aim at the f7-pawn.} 6.Bb3 6...Bg4 {Black can also try to pin our knight. There is pressure on d4 now. Therefore we'll capture on e5.} 7.dxe5 7...Nxe5 {The knight recaptures, and our knight is attacked once again. But we have a tactic. We will capture on e5 and sacrifice our queen!} 8.Nxe5 8...Bxd1 {Black accepts the queen. And we'll take on f7 with check. In this forcing combination, we will actually be able to get the queen back.} 9.Bxf7+ 9...Ke7 {Black has to move the king to e7. And we've got a fork on c6.} 10.Nc6+ 10...Kxf7 {Black takes the bishop. But we'l get the queen back with check.} 11.Nxd8+ 11...Rxd8 {Naturally, Black captures the knight.} 12.Kxd1 {After all is said and done, we are up a pawn and have an advantage. Both sides will look to finish development next. We should be careful of Black pushing ...d5, in which case we will go e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...b5 {In nearly 70% of the games, Black pushes ...b5 here and kicks out our bishop from a4. We retreat to b3, from where we aim at the f7-pawn.} 6.Bb3 6...Nxd4 {Black immediately goes after the d4-pawn. We should always trade knights on d4, since it clears the path for e5 in the future.} 7.Nxd4 7...exd4 {Black recaptures the piece. They're setting up the Noah's Ark Trap if we take with Qxd4. Now we'll push c3 to threaten the d4-pawn. We're the ones who are better prepared!} 8.c3 8...dxc3 {Black gets rid of it... which helps us develop the knight to its natural square.} 9.Nxc3 9...Nf6 {Black begins to develop their kingside. Now we'll successfully push e5, which is always a good sign.} 10.e5 10...dxe5 {Black doesn't want to be passive and trades. Now we have a deflection tactic! Black cannot capture the bishop, as their queen would be hanging.} 11.Bxf7+ 11...Ke7 {This is the only reasonable move.} 12.Bd5 {By retreating the bishop we avoid the queen trade and retain a healthy grip over the central light-squares. We can try to put some pressure on Black's king since it is stranded in the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...b5 {In nearly 70% of the games, Black pushes ...b5 here and kicks out our bishop from a4. We retreat to b3, from where we aim at the f7-pawn.} 6.Bb3 6...exd4 {To get rid of the pressure we put on the e5-pawn, Black trades on d4. It's time to get our king into safety.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Instead, Black develops the bishop. Our bishop on d5 once again pierces through the weakened light-squares on the queenside.} 8.Bd5 8...Bb7 {Black defends with the bishop coming to b7.} 9.Nxd4 {And now we add even more pressure to the knight by grabbing the d4-pawn. We have a healthy advantage and will increase pressure on the position after 9...Qd7 10.a4, for example.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...b5 {In nearly 70% of the games, Black pushes ...b5 here and kicks out our bishop from a4. We retreat to b3, from where we aim at the f7-pawn.} 6.Bb3 6...exd4 {To get rid of the pressure we put on the e5-pawn, Black trades on d4. It's time to get our king into safety.} 7.O-O 7...Nf6 {Black is finally starting to develop their kingside.} 8.Ng5 {...but after Ng5, the f7-pawn is very hard to defend. We'll most likely win it if Black doesn't go ...Be6, giving up material on the e6-square. White has an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...b5 {In nearly 70% of the games, Black pushes ...b5 here and kicks out our bishop from a4. We retreat to b3, from where we aim at the f7-pawn.} 6.Bb3 6...exd4 {To get rid of the pressure we put on the e5-pawn, Black trades on d4. It's time to get our king into safety.} 7.O-O 7...Bg4 {Instead, Black pins our knight on f3. Let's use this plan of challenging the d4-pawn with c3. However, here it comes with a tactical surprise...} 8.c3 8...dxc3 {Black usually trades. This gives us a powerful queen move. Firstly we attack two weakened points: the undefended knight and the pawn on f7.} 9.Qd5 9...Qd7 {Black has only this move to defend both of them.} 10.Nd4 {After Nd4, Black's position slowly falls apart. The knight cannot be defended, and Black must most likely sacrifice the exchange with ...Nxd4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {In 13% of the games, Black trades in the center immediately before pushing ...b5. We'll recapture with the knight, and now we have two pieces attacking the c6-knight.} 6.Nxd4 6...Bd7 {Black defends c6 with the bishop. We'll trade on c6, intending to double up Black's c-pawns.} 7.Nxc6 7...Bxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll trade again. Remember, our plan is to damage the queenside pawn structure.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures the piece. Now we'll start developing our queenside pieces.} 9.Nc3 9...Nf6 {Black develops in a similar manner.} 10.Qd3 {And after Qd3, we'll have a plan of fianchettoing with b3 and Bb2 to finish the queenside development. We will castle queenside and we can push our kingside pawns up the board with moves like f4 and e5 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...a6 and 4...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/8e4dd050-a6a6-4944-b8c2-a16144928743/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We develop the knight and hit the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black brings out the knight to c6 and supports e5. Our bishop gets deployed to b5 from where it puts some pressure on the knight.} 3.Bb5 3...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. The combination of 3...a6 and 4...d6 is called the Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Variation. The bishop usually retreats to a4.} 4.Ba4 4...d6 {In 9% of the games, Black defends their e5-pawn with ...d6. The...d6 idea is better for Black in combination with... a6 since it gives them ideas of ...b5. Nevertheless, it's still a passive move that closes down the dark-squared bishop. We'll stick to our aggressive idea and push d4 here.} 5.d4 5...Bd7 {In 12% of the games, Black immediately breaks the pin with ...Bd7. This isn't a very active move... ...and we can gain some central space with d5.} 6.d5 6...Nce7 {Black retreats the knight.} 7.c4 {With c4 we fortify the central structure and continue to develop while enjoying a nice space advantage. We don't mind trading bishops with Bxd7+ because we have pawns on light squares. Besides this, we will play Nc3 and 0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...Nxd4 {Black has less space, so trading is logical for them. Firstly, we'll trade on d7 with check before recapturing the knight.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Qxd7 {Black takes. Now we'll regain the material. Our queen sits nicely on d4.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nf6 {Black starts to develop their kingside. Developing pieces with a threat is always a great idea. This also frees up the queenside so we can castle there.} 9.Bg5 9...Be7 {Black defends the knight on f6. Castling queenside is perfect here. We gain more control over the d-file and connect the rooks while getting our king into safety.} 10.O-O-O 10...O-O {Black castles on the opposite side. Using the fact that Black's queen on d7 is insufficiently protected, we can get our important e5-move in!} 11.e5 11...Ne8 {Black has to retreat the knight. We'll trade off the dark-squared bishops, drawing the black queen to a square where we can gain tempo on it with our knight.} 12.Bxe7 12...Qxe7 {Black recaptures. By trading on d6 we unleash our powerful queen-rook combo along the d-file.} 13.exd6 13...Nxd6 {The knight recaptures on d6.} 14.Nd5 {Nd5 threatens the queen an threatens to capture on c7, with Qxd6 next. The open files for our heavy pieces prove to be advantageous here. Stockfish gives this position a +2 evaluation: this means it's borderline winning and that the Steinitz Defense isn't that good.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...Nxd4 {Black has less space, so trading is logical for them. Firstly, we'll trade on d7 with check before recapturing the knight.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Qxd7 {Black takes. Now we'll regain the material. Our queen sits nicely on d4.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nf6 {Black starts to develop their kingside. Developing pieces with a threat is always a great idea. This also frees up the queenside so we can castle there.} 9.Bg5 9...Be7 {Black defends the knight on f6. Castling queenside is perfect here. We gain more control over the d-file and connect the rooks while getting our king into safety.} 10.O-O-O 10...O-O {Black castles on the opposite side. Using the fact that Black's queen on d7 is insufficiently protected, we can get our important e5-move in!} 11.e5 11...Ne8 {Black has to retreat the knight. We'll trade off the dark-squared bishops, drawing the black queen to a square where we can gain tempo on it with our knight.} 12.Bxe7 12...Qxe7 {Black recaptures. By trading on d6 we unleash our powerful queen-rook combo along the d-file.} 13.exd6 13...Qxd6 {Alternatively Black might offer a trade of queens and try to avoid the impact of Nd5. We'll still trade.} 14.Qxd6 14...Nxd6 {Black recaptures.} 15.Nd5 {The c7-pawn will fall, since it cannot be defended. A Ne7+ fork is in the air! We are virtually winning outright.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...Nxd4 {Black has less space, so trading is logical for them. Firstly, we'll trade on d7 with check before recapturing the knight.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Qxd7 {Black takes. Now we'll regain the material. Our queen sits nicely on d4.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nf6 {Black starts to develop their kingside. Developing pieces with a threat is always a great idea. This also frees up the queenside so we can castle there.} 9.Bg5 9...Be7 {Black defends the knight on f6. Castling queenside is perfect here. We gain more control over the d-file and connect the rooks while getting our king into safety.} 10.O-O-O 10...h6 {Black can attack our bishop. We'll take the knight with a specific idea in mind.} 11.Bxf6 11...Bxf6 {Once Black recaptures, we have a strong tactic. And that's e5! We attack the bishop on f6 and threaten to take on d6 as well. Black can't capture with the pawn because it's pinned.} 12.e5 12...Bxe5 {But what if they capture with the bishop?} 13.Rhe1 {We can pin it as well! Black is busted here because they are pinned on multiple fronts and won't be able to save the bishop. If ...f6, we have f4. If O-O, we can just take the bishop, and if O-O-O, we win with Rxe5 dxe5 Qxa7 threatening both mate and the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {With 22% popularity, Black brings out the knight instead of trading on d4. By trading off the c6-knight, we get to keep our strong knight on d4.} 7.Bxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn to preserve their bishop. f3 is a great square for the queen. It eyeballs the c6-pawn.} 8.Qf3 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Even here, the idea of e5 is very strong! This unleashes an attack on the c6-pawn.} 9.e5 9...dxe5 {Black accepts the e-pawn.} 10.Nxc6 {We'll grab the c-pawn, which nicely ties in with our queen on f3. Black's queenside has some severe weaknesses. Be3 and Rd1 are good moves to follow.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {With 22% popularity, Black brings out the knight instead of trading on d4. By trading off the c6-knight, we get to keep our strong knight on d4.} 7.Bxc6 7...Bxc6 {If Black goes for ...Bxc6...} 8.Qd3 {...we don't capture it, but develop instead with Qd3, preparing to castle queenside after Bf4. We have strong and easy moves like f3 and h4, h5 storming the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. As usual, we'll trade off on c6 to preserve the strong d4-knight.} 7.Bxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll get our king into safety.} 8.O-O 8...Nf6 {Black wants to do so as well, so they start developing their kingside pieces. f3 is the standard square for our queen. Our target is the c6-pawn.} 9.Qf3 9...Be7 {...Be7 prepares to castle. Similarly to the previous line, e5 should be played to open up an attack on the c6-pawn.} 10.e5 10...dxe5 {Black captures on e5.} 11.Nxc6 {Yet again, the knight poses problems to Black on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Steinitz Defense (3...d6) / Study 2: Black plays 3...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/53e7faf0-9f2d-405b-87e4-e88aca1ba749/studies/9a7a012c-109f-4cb8-9551-d106bc10acd8/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight and start to put pressure on the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops as well. The bishop goes to b5 as usual.} 3.Bb5 3...d6 {This move is popular at the club level with 22% occurrence. Black's idea is to open up the light-squared bishop and bring immediate support to the e5-pawn. This move is passive, which means that d4 is even stronger here. We immediately pose some threats, either gaining space with d5 or winning e5.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black releases the pressure by trading on d4. We'll recapture with the knight and threaten c6.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bd7 {Black defends it and unpins. Now it's time to start developing other pieces. The knight usually ends up on d5 which is a great square for it. We also defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...a6 {Black asks our bishop a question. As usual, we'll trade off on c6 to preserve the strong d4-knight.} 7.Bxc6 7...Bxc6 {If Black captures with the bishop...} 8.Qd3 {... Qd3 initiates the plan of Bf4 and O-O-O. Our space advantage affords us an edge in the evaluation. f3 can be played to reinforce the center, and the expansion with h4 will give us a great attack on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/7dcd9c86-9818-4c82-aa4e-c144a8b9cc1f/studies/419b504b-bfe9-4881-af9e-205091f01e12/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...Nxe4 {Black's second option is to capture the e4-pawn with their knight. This is played a quarter of the time. Against this, we'll bring our rook to the e-file and attack Black's knight.} 5.Re1 5...Nd6 {Black moves their knight out of danger and attacks our bishop. And we'll capture the e5-pawn. We are now threatening some nasty discoveries. For example, ...Nxb5 would now fail to Nxc6+.} 6.Nxe5 6...Nxe5 {Usually, Black captures the knight. This happens over half the time. We'll recapture with our rook.} 7.Rxe5+ 7...Be7 {Black then blocks the check with their bishop and prepares to castle. And now an important move, Bf1! The d6-knight was threatening to take our bishop, so we bring it all the way back to its home square.} 8.Bf1 8...O-O {Black moves their king to safety. We'll advance our d-pawn, taking some central space and opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 9.d4 9...Bf6 {And Black goes ...Bf6, activating their bishop and attacking our rook. This is where things get spicy... Instead of retreating the rook, we develop our light-squared bishop! Our idea here is to sacrifice the exchange in order to get some attacking chances on the kingside.} 10.Bd3 10...g6 {Instead of taking the rook, Black can also go ...g6, with the idea to blunt our light-squared bishop. Against ...g6, I suggest we develop our knight to c3, with the intention of jumping it into the d5-square.} 11.Nc3 11...c6 {Black goes ...c6, covering d5. Now we'll develop our dark-squared bishop and attack Black's rook.} 12.Bh6 12...Bg7 {Black goes ...Bg7, offering a trade of bishops. We'll exchange.} 13.Bxg7 13...Kxg7 {And when Black recaptures...} 14.Qf3 {We bring our queen out to f3. This is an excellent position for White. We are better developed, our pieces are more active and we've also got a lot more space. We can look to double rooks and break with d5 at the right moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/7dcd9c86-9818-4c82-aa4e-c144a8b9cc1f/studies/419b504b-bfe9-4881-af9e-205091f01e12/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...Bc5 {Black develops their dark-squared bishop to the most active square. This is played 26% of the time. Now we've got a nice tactic - we can grab the e5-pawn!} 5.Nxe5 5...Nxe5 {Black captures our knight. And now we'll advance our d-pawn, forking Black's bishop and knight!} 6.d4 6...Bxd4 {Black usually takes the d-pawn. We'll take the bishop with our queen, attacking Black's knight.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nc6 {Black moves their knight out of danger and attacks our queen. We'll move our queen back a square. This tactical sequence was really just a trade of material, but we're now left with a strong central pawn.} 8.Qd3 8...O-O {Black usually castles their king.} 9.Nc3 {And we simply develop our knight. White is better. We've got more space, open lines, and great development. We'll proceed by developing our dark-squared bishop and bringing our rooks to the central files.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/7dcd9c86-9818-4c82-aa4e-c144a8b9cc1f/studies/419b504b-bfe9-4881-af9e-205091f01e12/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...Nxe4 {Black's second option is to capture the e4-pawn with their knight. This is played a quarter of the time. Against this, we'll bring our rook to the e-file and attack Black's knight.} 5.Re1 5...Nd6 {Black moves their knight out of danger and attacks our bishop. And we'll capture the e5-pawn. We are now threatening some nasty discoveries. For example, ...Nxb5 would now fail to Nxc6+.} 6.Nxe5 6...Nxe5 {Usually, Black captures the knight. This happens over half the time. We'll recapture with our rook.} 7.Rxe5+ 7...Be7 {Black then blocks the check with their bishop and prepares to castle. And now an important move, Bf1! The d6-knight was threatening to take our bishop, so we bring it all the way back to its home square.} 8.Bf1 8...O-O {Black moves their king to safety. We'll advance our d-pawn, taking some central space and opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 9.d4 9...Bf6 {And Black goes ...Bf6, activating their bishop and attacking our rook. This is where things get spicy... Instead of retreating the rook, we develop our light-squared bishop! Our idea here is to sacrifice the exchange in order to get some attacking chances on the kingside.} 10.Bd3 10...Bxe5 {Usually, Black takes the rook. This happens 86% of the time! We'll recapture with our pawn.} 11.dxe5 11...Ne8 {Black is forced to retreat their knight to the only safe square.} 12.Nc3 {And we develop our knight. Objectively speaking, this position is balanced, but Black can easily go wrong and the position is much easier to play from the White side. We've got great attacking potential and our queen can quickly start going after Black's kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
