1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...Nxe4 {Black's second option is to capture the e4-pawn with their knight. This is played a quarter of the time. Against this, we'll bring our rook to the e-file and attack Black's knight.} 5.Re1 5...Nd6 {Black moves their knight out of danger and attacks our bishop. And we'll capture the e5-pawn. We are now threatening some nasty discoveries. For example, ...Nxb5 would now fail to Nxc6+.} 6.Nxe5 6...Nxe5 {Usually, Black captures the knight. This happens over half the time. We'll recapture with our rook.} 7.Rxe5+ 7...Be7 {Black then blocks the check with their bishop and prepares to castle. And now an important move, Bf1! The d6-knight was threatening to take our bishop, so we bring it all the way back to its home square.} 8.Bf1 8...O-O {Black moves their king to safety. We'll advance our d-pawn, taking some central space and opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 9.d4 9...Bf6 {And Black goes ...Bf6, activating their bishop and attacking our rook. This is where things get spicy... Instead of retreating the rook, we develop our light-squared bishop! Our idea here is to sacrifice the exchange in order to get some attacking chances on the kingside.} 10.Bd3 10...Re8 {Black can also not take the rook and instead offer a trade of rooks. Against this, we should simply develop our dark-squared bishop.} 11.Bf4 11...Bxe5 {If Black now takes the rook...} 12.dxe5 {We can recapture with the pawn and the black knight is trapped!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/7dcd9c86-9818-4c82-aa4e-c144a8b9cc1f/studies/419b504b-bfe9-4881-af9e-205091f01e12/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...Nxe4 {Black's second option is to capture the e4-pawn with their knight. This is played a quarter of the time. Against this, we'll bring our rook to the e-file and attack Black's knight.} 5.Re1 5...Nd6 {Black moves their knight out of danger and attacks our bishop. And we'll capture the e5-pawn. We are now threatening some nasty discoveries. For example, ...Nxb5 would now fail to Nxc6+.} 6.Nxe5 6...Be7 {Black's other option is to develop their bishop and prepare castling. Against this, we should bring our bishop back to its home square. If Black now takes the e5-knight, we transpose into the main line.} 7.Bf1 7...O-O {Black moves their king to safety.} 8.d4 {And we push our d-pawn, taking space and supporting our knight. We've got more space and a position that's easy to play. We can proceed by supporting our d-pawn and developing our dark-squared bishop to the f4-square. If Black ever takes our knight, we should recapture with the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Berlin Defense (3...Nf6) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/7dcd9c86-9818-4c82-aa4e-c144a8b9cc1f/studies/419b504b-bfe9-4881-af9e-205091f01e12/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and defends the e5-pawn. We'll go for the Ruy Lopez by developing our bishop to b5.} 3.Bb5 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight and attacks our central pawn. This happens 18% of the time and characterizes the Berlin Defense. We'll respond by ignoring the attack and castling our king. By castling, we're getting ready to bring our rook over to the e-file. Black now has three options...} 4.O-O 4...d6 {Black's third option is to passively support their e5-pawn with ...d6. This is played 24% of the time. Against this, we'll advance our d-pawn and threaten to take on e5.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black usually captures the pawn. Now we'll recapture with our knight, putting pressure on the c6-knight.} 6.Nxd4 6...Bd7 {Black goes ...Bd7 in order to support and unpin the knight.} 7.Nc3 {And we develop our other knight. This position is very comfortable for White. We've got more space and our development is superior. b3 and Bb2 is a good plan in some lines, but if Nxd4 you already know that Bxd7+ comes first, before recapturing on d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...d6 {Black could go for the more sensible ...d6, played in 30% of existing games in the database. It is also the computer's top choice. On b5, the bishop was no longer useful, so we'll retreat it to c4 and slice through the kingside light squares.} 7.Bc4 7...Bg4 {Black pins our knight. We'll ask it a question.} 8.h3 {We ask it a question.} 8...Bh5 {Black sensibly retreats to h5 and maintains the pin. It's time to start developing the queenside pieces, starting with the knight.} 9.Nc3 9...Qd7 {Black wants to castle queenside. Our bishop starts to aim there so that a future attack could be possible.} 10.Be3 10...O-O-O {Black castles.} 11.Rb1 {Opposide sides castling always means that both sides will launch some sort of an attack. Rb1 initiates a simple plan; b4 and b5 to attack on the queenside!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bc5 {Black develops actively. More than 50% of games go like this. We'll bring the queen to d3 where it not only supports the e4-pawn, but could also shift to the c4-square with check in case Black castles immediately.} 7.Qd3 7...d6 {Black supports the bishop. Let's be a little bit annoying here and prevent Black from castling.} 8.Qc4 8...Qe7 {Black wants to untangle with ...Be6. We'll develop the knight to c3 and aim for the d5-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Be6 {Our queen is under attack now. But we have a way to secure the advantage. We'll occupy the d5-square and block the attack, while attacking the black queen in return.} 10.Nd5 10...Qf7 {Black moves their queen...} 11.Nxc7+ {...but now we have a nice tactic of capturing on c7 with check, winning a pawn and infiltrating Black's position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bc5 {Black develops actively. More than 50% of games go like this. We'll bring the queen to d3 where it not only supports the e4-pawn, but could also shift to the c4-square with check in case Black castles immediately.} 7.Qd3 7...d6 {Black supports the bishop. Let's be a little bit annoying here and prevent Black from castling.} 8.Qc4 8...Qe7 {Black wants to untangle with ...Be6. We'll develop the knight to c3 and aim for the d5-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Bd7 {Black frees up the queenside. We'll gain a tempo on the queen.} 10.Nd5 {The knight is still strong on d5. Our pieces are a little bit more active than Black's. 10...Nxd5 is an only move, because 10...Qd8 11.b4 Bb6 12.Bg5 is too much pressure on the knight on f6. 10...Nxd5 11.exd5 gives us a strong pawn on d5, with easy development to follow. c3 and b4 are ideas there.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...Nf6 {In 30% of the games, Black develops the knight instead and aims to target the e4-pawn. King safety is much more important here, so we'll castle.} 5.O-O 5...Bc5 {Grabbing the pawn on e4 would be dangerous due to the e-file opening up, so Black develops the bishop to c5 instead. The pawn on f5 is undefended at the moment but, most importantly, we open the center since Black's king hasn't castled yet.} 6.exf5 6...O-O {Now Black gets castled. e4 looks like a great outpost for our knight!} 7.Nbd2 7...d6 {Black opens up an attack on the f5-pawn. Our knight jumps to e4 and attacks the bishop on c5. Winning Black's dark-squared bishop would be crucial, as it is Black's strongest piece. Having the bishop pair in an open position would definitely benefit White.} 8.Ne4 8...Bb6 {Black keeps the bishop.} 9.Ng3 {But now we get to keep the f5-pawn! It is hard for our opponent to add attackers to it. And we can always add a defender with if ...Ne7 with Nh4. c3 for Ba4 and Bc2 is also a plan.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bc5 {Black develops actively. More than 50% of games go like this. We'll bring the queen to d3 where it not only supports the e4-pawn, but could also shift to the c4-square with check in case Black castles immediately.} 7.Qd3 7...O-O {If Black castles here...} 8.Qc4+ {...we've got Qc4+ to pick up the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...d6 {Black could go for the more sensible ...d6, played in 30% of existing games in the database. It is also the computer's top choice. On b5, the bishop was no longer useful, so we'll retreat it to c4 and slice through the kingside light squares.} 7.Bc4 7...Na5 {Black goes after our strong light-squared bishop. We should definitely retreat it and keep it.} 8.Be2 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Note that ...Nxe4 is impossible due to Qd5. The knight is misplaced on a5, so let's try to gain some tempi on it, starting with b4.} 9.b4 9...Nc6 {Black retreats it. Let's kick it away one more time.} 10.b5 10...Na5 {Black doesn't want to be too passive and retreats to a5 instead of b8. Now we'll develop and attack it again.} 11.Bd2 11...b6 {Black protects it with a pawn. We'll trade it off in order to damage Black's pawn structure on the queenside.} 12.Bxa5 12...bxa5 {Black recaptures.} 13.Bc4 {And the bishop on c4 is a great piece, which doesn't allow Black to castle and get their king into safety. White has an advantage, and the moves that follow flow naturally: Nc3, Qd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bc5 {Black develops actively. More than 50% of games go like this. We'll bring the queen to d3 where it not only supports the e4-pawn, but could also shift to the c4-square with check in case Black castles immediately.} 7.Qd3 7...d6 {Black supports the bishop. Let's be a little bit annoying here and prevent Black from castling.} 8.Qc4 8...Bd7 {Black is trying to untangle with ...Bd7, ...Qe7 and perhaps castle queenside. We have to be quick here, so the knight jumps to c3 and takes aim at the d5-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Qe7 {Black frees up the queenside. We'll gain a tempo on the queen.} 10.Nd5 {The knight is still strong on d5. Our pieces are a little bit more active than Black's. 10...Nxd5 is an only move, because 10...Qd8 11.b4 Bb6 12.Bg5 is too much pressure on the knight on f6. 10...Nxd5 11.exd5 gives us a strong pawn on d5, with easy development to follow. c3 and b4 are ideas there.} 10...Nxd5 {Black trades off our strong knight. We'll recapture with the pawn, which also comes with a tempo.} 11.exd5 11...Nd4 {Black saves the knight. Without the light-squared bishop, Black will have a hard time defending the light squares, so we'll exchange on d7.} 12.Bxd7+ 12...Qxd7 {The queen recaptures. Black was threatening a trade on f3, so we'll get rid of their active knight.} 13.Nxd4 13...Bxd4 {The bishop recaptures. Gaining space on the queenside is strong here. a4 threatens to trap the d4-bishop with our pawns.} 14.a4 14...a6 {Black makes room for their bishop to retreat if attacked by the white pawns. Instead, we'll offer to trade off the bishop and aim to open up our f1-rook.} 15.Be3 15...Bxe3 {Black goes for it, since there isn't a better option.} 16.fxe3 {We recapture. The pawn on d5 provides the space advantage and our play will revolve around controlling the f-file by doubling up the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...Nf6 {In 30% of the games, Black develops the knight instead and aims to target the e4-pawn. King safety is much more important here, so we'll castle.} 5.O-O 5...Bc5 {Grabbing the pawn on e4 would be dangerous due to the e-file opening up, so Black develops the bishop to c5 instead. The pawn on f5 is undefended at the moment but, most importantly, we open the center since Black's king hasn't castled yet.} 6.exf5 6...d6 {Black tries to immediately recapture the f5-pawn. Black didn't castle, so we open up the center. This comes with a tempo and will allow our knight to get into a better square.} 7.d4 7...exd4 {Black takes.} 8.Nxd4 {We recapture and add pressure to the c6-knight while protecting f5 and Re1+ coming. We have a small advantage. The best move for Black is 8...Bxd4, but after 9.Qxd4 we have the important dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...Nxe4 {Black is greedy and goes for the e4-pawn. While this isn't so popular, some may wonder why not take the free pawn. This is practically losing. We'll attack it with Re1.} 7.Re1 7...d5 {Black supports the knight with a pawn. We'll play Nc3, adding pressure to both the d-pawn and the e4-knight. There's also e-file pressure. Black has made a mistake.} 8.Nc3 8...Nxc3 {Black trades to relieve the pressure.} 9.bxc3 {After we recapture, Black has some hanging pawns in the center and their king doesn't feel safe either. 9...e4 10.Ne5 will be followed by moves like Bf4 and Black is guaranteed a bad time. The engine gives us a +2 advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 1: The Schliemann Defense, 3...f5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/95cbc3f6-7929-4a60-9005-9ac3f7689c89/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black mirrors us with ...e5. We'll develop our knight as before.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops also. The Ruy Lopez starts with 3.Bb5.} 3.Bb5 3...f5 {3...f5 is not very popular, as it occurs in only 2% of the games. It's a very aggressive approach by Black, immediately attacking our e4-pawn and aiming to spice things up in the center on their own terms. A simple, yet strong solution is to just protect e4 with another pawn. Keeping a pawn in the center is crucial here to limit Black's play.} 4.d3 4...fxe4 {Black trades, as they do in more than half of the games. We'll recapture.} 5.dxe4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, but now their light-squares are weakened due to the absence of the f-pawn. We'll castle and get our king into safety.} 6.O-O 6...d6 {Black could go for the more sensible ...d6, played in 30% of existing games in the database. It is also the computer's top choice. On b5, the bishop was no longer useful, so we'll retreat it to c4 and slice through the kingside light squares.} 7.Bc4 7...Bg4 {Black pins our knight. We'll ask it a question.} 8.h3 {We ask it a question.} 8...Bh5 {Black sensibly retreats to h5 and maintains the pin. It's time to start developing the queenside pieces, starting with the knight.} 9.Nc3 9...Nd4 {Black centralizes the knight to d4 instead and attacks our f3-knight.} 10.g4 {We can safely push g4 here. Black must trade on f3 (otherwise they'll lose material on d4) which will activate our queen and allow us to put pressure on the king with g5 and h4 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bc5, 3...Nge7, or 3...Nd4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/4e2c3a21-2f45-4bdd-bd01-02d3e8c29c43/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Our opponent responds in a similar fashion. We'll develop and target the e-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. We'll put the Ruy Lopez on the board.} 3.Bb5 3...Bc5 {This move occurs in 12% of the games. Black brings out the bishop to c5 and aims at our soft f2-pawn. Against this, c3 with a quick d4 coming is a strong approach.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black gets their other knight out. We can expand with d4 and attack Black's bishop.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades.} 6.e5 {And the thematic e5-move is played here as well. We'll castle and capture on d4 later if allowed. 6...Ne4 7.0-0 dxc3 8.Qd5 is good for White. If this variation isn't played, Black will still have a tough time because cxd4 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bc5, 3...Nge7, or 3...Nd4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/4e2c3a21-2f45-4bdd-bd01-02d3e8c29c43/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Our opponent responds in a similar fashion. We'll develop and target the e-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. We'll put the Ruy Lopez on the board.} 3.Bb5 3...Nge7 {This is a very rare move, played in 5% of the games. Black reinforces their knight on c6, but the downside is that they block their dark-squared bishop. Let's play actively here, and strike in the center with d4.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nxd4 {Black trades yet again. We'll take the piece back.} 6.Qxd4 6...c6 {Black threatens our bishop, but blocks the natural square for their knight.} 7.Be2 {We simply retreat. We has good control over the center with natural developing moves coming. Nc3, Be3, and even 0-0-0 are good moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bc5, 3...Nge7, or 3...Nd4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/4e2c3a21-2f45-4bdd-bd01-02d3e8c29c43/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Our opponent responds in a similar fashion. We'll develop and target the e-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. We'll put the Ruy Lopez on the board.} 3.Bb5 3...Nge7 {This is a very rare move, played in 5% of the games. Black reinforces their knight on c6, but the downside is that they block their dark-squared bishop. Let's play actively here, and strike in the center with d4.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nxd4 {Black trades yet again. We'll take the piece back.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nc6 {6...Nc6 attacks our queen. Black opens up their bishop and invites us to play Bxc6.} 7.Qe3 {But we will retreat the queen to e3, castle, and develop the queenside pieces. 0-0, Nc3, Rd1 and even Nd5 will be our moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Ruy Lopez: Move 3 Alternatives / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bc5, 3...Nge7, or 3...Nd4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8dc7622e-f87f-4937-86ad-c77f29584b69/chapters/43c26138-f2c1-4970-b204-ebe396a445bc/studies/4e2c3a21-2f45-4bdd-bd01-02d3e8c29c43/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Our opponent responds in a similar fashion. We'll develop and target the e-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also brings out the knight. We'll put the Ruy Lopez on the board.} 3.Bb5 3...Nd4 {Yet another rare move is 3...Nd4, attacking our light-squared bishop and occurring in 6% of the games. The database names this the Bird Variation. Nevertheless, moving the same piece twice in the opening is usually not good. We'll trade off the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...exd4 {Black recaptures. We'll get our king to safety, while Black's development is lacking.} 5.O-O 5...c6 {They attack our bishop... ...which retreats to c4, eyeing the f7-pawn.} 6.Bc4 6...d5 {Black expands in the center. Let's not allow Black to get ahead by gaining control over the center, so we'll trade on d5.} 7.exd5 7...cxd5 {Black has to recapture with the pawn. We'll give a check with the bishop! Simply retreating would allow Black to gain some time and develop, but this way we don't let them.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop. We'll give another check, this time with the rook. Quickly developing with tempo is a great idea against Black's lack of development.} 9.Re1+ 9...Be7 {Black has to block again. 9...Ne7 was the only move according to the engine, but people want to hang onto hopes of ...Nf6 and ...0-0.} 10.Qg4 {...and here comes the powerful Qg4. Black has a hard time defending the g7-pawn and finding coordination between their pieces. They are lost. We will take on g7 if allowed, or move onto taking the d-pawns.} *